[270.678463, "o", "Keys in root: ['answers']\r\nNumber of answers: 8\r\n"]
[270.678505, "o", "\r\nAnswer 1:\r\n  qid: b2531657ba5c7a78\r\n  status: ANSWERED\r\n  answer length: 548\r\n  citations count: 3\r\n    - PASSAGE_0000 (BOXED WARNING)\r\n      Quote: 'PAXLOVID includes ritonavir, a strong CYP3A inhibitor, which may lead to greater exposure of certain concomitant medications, resulting in potentially severe, life-threatening, or fatal events'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0013 (5.1 Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions)\r\n      Quote: 'The most commonly reported concomitant medications resulting in serious adverse reactions were calcineurin inhibitors (e.g., tacrolimus, cyclosporine), followed by calcium channel blockers.'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0019 (7.1 Potential for PAXLOVID to Affect Other Drugs)\r\n      Quote: 'PAXLOVID (nirmatrelvir co-packaged with ritonavir) is a strong inhibitor of CYP3A'\r\n\r\nAnswer 2:\r\n  qid: 6f12607a1ba14dd5\r\n  status: ANSWERED\r\n  answer length: 930\r\n  citations count: 1\r\n    - PASSAGE_0012 (4 CONTRAINDICATIONS)\r\n      Quote: 'PAXLOVID is contraindicated with drugs that are primarily metabolized by CYP3A and for which elevated concentrations are associated with serious and/or life-threatening reactions and drugs that are strong CYP3A inducers where significantly reduced nirmatrelvir or ritonavir plasma concentrations may be associated with the potential for loss of virologic response and possible resistance.'\r\n"]
[270.678569, "o", "\r\nAnswer 3:\r\n  qid: daef650b92e56473\r\n  status: ANSWERED\r\n  answer length: 459\r\n  citations count: 3\r\n    - PASSAGE_0001 (2.1 General Administration Recommendations)\r\n      Quote: 'Lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir oral solution must be taken with food.'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0002 (2.2 Administering Oral Solution by Feeding Tube)\r\n      Quote: 'Because lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution contains ethanol and propylene glycol, it is not recommended for use with polyurethane feeding tubes due to potential incompatibility. Feeding tubes that are compatible with ethanol and propylene glycol, such as silicone and polyvinyl chloride (PVC) feeding tubes, can be used for administration of lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution.'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0106 (11 DESCRIPTION)\r\n      Quote: 'Lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution contains approximately 42% (v/v) ethanol and approximately 15% (w/v) propylene glycol.'\r\n\r\nAnswer 4:\r\n  qid: a0576a26f2a2eeac\r\n  status: ANSWERED\r\n  answer length: 796\r\n  citations count: 5\r\n    - PASSAGE_0003 (2.3 Dosage Recommendations in Adults)\r\n      Quote: 'Lopinavir and ritonavir can be given in once daily or twice daily dosing regimen at dosages noted in Tables 1 and 2. Lopinavir and ritonavir once daily dosing regimen is not recommended in:'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0004 (Recommended Dosage)\r\n      Quote: '80 mg/20 mg per mL Oral Solution 800 mg/200 mg (10 mL) once daily'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0005 (Recommended Dosage)\r\n      Quote: '80 mg/20 mg per mL Oral Solution 400 mg/100 mg (5 mL)\\xa0twice daily'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0006 (Recommended Dosage)\r\n      Quote: '80 mg/20 mg per mL Oral Solution 520 mg/130 mg (6.5 mL) twice daily'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0012 (2.5 Dosage Recommendations in Pregnancy)\r\n      Quote: 'Administer 400/100 mg of lopinavir and ritonavir twice daily in pregnant patients with no documented lopinavir-associated resistance substitutions.'\r\n\r\nAnswer 5:\r\n  qid: 9be5515bc3064979\r\n  status: ANSWERED\r\n  answer length: 845\r\n  citations count: 7\r\n    - PASSAGE_0014 (5.1 Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions)\r\n      Quote: 'Initiation of lopinavir and ritonavir, a CYP3A inhibitor, in patients receiving medications metabolized by CYP3A or initiation of medications metabolized by CYP3A in patients already receiving lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir, may increase plasma concentrations of medications metabolized by CYP3A.'\r\n"]
[270.678589, "o", "    - PASSAGE_0015 (5.2 Toxicity in Preterm Neonates)\r\n      Quote: 'Preterm neonates may be at increased risk of propylene glycol-associated adverse events due to diminished ability to metabolize propylene glycol, thereby leading to accumulation and potential adverse events.'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0016 (5.3 Pancreatitis)\r\n      Quote: 'Pancreatitis has been observed in patients receiving lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir therapy, including those who developed marked triglyceride elevations.'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0017 (5.4 Hepatotoxicity)\r\n      Quote: 'There have been postmarketing reports of hepatic dysfunction, including some fatalities. These have generally occurred in patients with advanced HIV-1 disease taking multiple concomitant medications in the setting of underlying chronic hepatitis or cirrhosis.'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0018 (5.5 QT Interval Prolongation)\r\n      Quote: 'Postmarketing cases of QT interval prolongation and torsade de pointes have been reported although causality of lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir could not be established.'\r\n"]
[270.678653, "o", "    - PASSAGE_0019 (5.6 PR Interval Prolongation)\r\n      Quote: 'Lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir prolongs the PR interval in some patients. Cases of second or third degree atrioventricular block have been reported.'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0022 (5.9 Lipid Elevations)\r\n      Quote: 'Treatment with lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir has resulted in large increases in the concentration of total cholesterol and triglycerides .'\r\n"]
[270.678722, "o", "\r\nAnswer 6:\r\n  qid: 18078efe9380c8dc\r\n  status: ANSWERED\r\n  answer length: 977\r\n  citations count: 7\r\n    - PASSAGE_0014 (5.1 Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions)\r\n      Quote: 'Initiation of lopinavir and ritonavir, a CYP3A inhibitor, in patients receiving medications metabolized by CYP3A or initiation of medications metabolized by CYP3A in patients already receiving lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir, may increase plasma concentrations of medications metabolized by CYP3A.'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0015 (5.2 Toxicity in Preterm Neonates)\r\n      Quote: 'Lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir oral solution should not be used in preterm neonates in the immediate postnatal period because of possible toxicities.'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0016 (5.3 Pancreatitis)\r\n      Quote: 'Pancreatitis should be considered if clinical symptoms (nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain) or abnormalities in laboratory values (such as increased serum lipase or amylase values) suggestive of pancreatitis occur.'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0017 (5.4 Hepatotoxicity)\r\n      Quote: 'Appropriate laboratory testing should be conducted prior to initiating therapy with lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir and patients should be monitored closely during treatment.'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0018 (5.5 QT Interval Prolongation)\r\n      Quote: 'Avoid use in patients with congenital long QT syndrome, those with hypokalemia, and with other drugs that prolong the QT interval .'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0019 (5.6 PR Interval Prolongation)\r\n      Quote: 'Lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir should be used with caution in patients with underlying structural heart disease, pre-existing conduction system abnormalities, ischemic heart disease or cardiomyopathies'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0022 (5.9 Lipid Elevations)\r\n      Quote: 'Triglyceride and cholesterol testing should be performed prior to initiating lopinavir\\xa0and ritonavir therapy and at periodic intervals during therapy.'\r\n\r\nAnswer 7:\r\n"]
[270.678796, "o", "  qid: fbab4114f66e2dda\r\n  status: ANSWERED\r\n  answer length: 670\r\n  citations count: 5\r\n    - PASSAGE_0000 (BOXED WARNING)\r\n      Quote: 'PAXLOVID includes ritonavir, a strong CYP3A inhibitor, which may lead to greater exposure of certain concomitant medications, resulting in potentially severe, life-threatening, or fatal events'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0013 (5.1 Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions)\r\n      Quote: 'Initiation of PAXLOVID, which contains ritonavir, a strong CYP3A inhibitor, in patients receiving medications metabolized by CYP3A or initiation of medications metabolized by CYP3A in patients already receiving PAXLOVID, may increase plasma concentrations of medications metabolized by CYP3A.'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0014 (5.2 Hypersensitivity Reactions)\r\n      Quote: 'Anaphylaxis, serious skin reactions (including toxic epidermal necrolysis and Stevens-Johnson syndrome), and other hypersensitivity reactions have been reported with PAXLOVID .'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0015 (5.3 Hepatotoxicity)\r\n      Quote: 'Hepatic transaminase elevations, clinical hepatitis, and jaundice have occurred in patients receiving ritonavir.'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0016 (5.4 Risk of HIV-1 Resistance Development)\r\n      Quote: 'Because nirmatrelvir is co-administered with ritonavir, there may be a risk of HIV-1 developing resistance to HIV protease inhibitors in individuals with uncontrolled or undiagnosed HIV-1 infection .'\r\n\r\nAnswer 8:"]
[270.678839, "o", "\r\n  qid: 2d63e0a82215867f\r\n"]
[270.678917, "o", "  status: ANSWERED\r\n  answer length: 739\r\n  citations count: 5\r\n    - PASSAGE_0013 (5.1 Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions)\r\n      Quote: 'Severe, life-threatening, and/or fatal adverse reactions due to drug interactions have been reported in patients treated with PAXLOVID.'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0014 (5.2 Hypersensitivity Reactions)\r\n      Quote: 'Anaphylaxis, serious skin reactions (including toxic epidermal necrolysis and Stevens-Johnson syndrome), and other hypersensitivity reactions have been reported with PAXLOVID .'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0015 (5.3 Hepatotoxicity)\r\n      Quote: 'Hepatic transaminase elevations, clinical hepatitis, and jaundice have occurred in patients receiving ritonavir.'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0016 (5.4 Risk of HIV-1 Resistance Development)\r\n      Quote: 'Because nirmatrelvir is co-administered with ritonavir, there may be a risk of HIV-1 developing resistance to HIV protease inhibitors in individuals with uncontrolled or undiagnosed HIV-1 infection .'\r\n    - PASSAGE_0018 (6.1 Clinical Trials Experience)\r\n      Quote: 'The most common adverse reactions (≥1% incidence in the PAXLOVID group and occurring at a greater frequency than in the placebo group) were dysgeusia (5% and <1%, respectively) and diarrhea (3% and 2%, respectively).'\r\n\r\nSuccess: All required QIDs are present and match perfectly!\r\n"]
